1. Distributed web infrastructure
==================================

Infrastructure diagram (whiteboard description):

                              [User]
                                |
                        DNS: www.foobar.com -> Load Balancer IP
                                |
                                v
                        [Load Balancer (HAproxy)]
                          /                \
                         /                  \
                        v                    v
                 [Server 1]            [Server 2]
                 - Nginx               - Nginx
                 - App server          - App server
                 - Codebase            - Codebase
                 - MySQL (Primary)     - MySQL (Replica)
                        \                    /
                         \                  /
                          Primary-Replica replication
                          (data written on Primary,
                           copied to Replica)

Explanation, starting from the user:

A user types www.foobar.com in their browser. DNS resolves this domain
to the IP address of the load balancer. The browser then sends an HTTP
request to that IP.

The load balancer (HAProxy) receives the request. Instead of forwarding
it to a single server, it distributes incoming traffic between Server 1
and Server 2, based on a distribution algorithm. Each of these two
servers is a full copy of the previous single-server setup: it has its
own web server (Nginx), application server, codebase, and database.

Why each additional element is added:

- Load balancer (HAProxy):
  Added to distribute traffic across multiple servers, so that no
  single server is overwhelmed, and to remove the single point of
  failure that existed with only one server. If one server goes down,
  the load balancer can redirect traffic to the remaining healthy
  server(s).

- A second server:
  Added for redundancy and to be able to handle more traffic
  (horizontal scaling). With two servers, if one fails, the other can
  keep serving users, and maintenance can be done on one server at a
  time without full downtime.

- Two databases (Primary-Replica):
  Added so that data is not lost if one database server fails, and so
  that read operations can be spread across multiple database
  instances, reducing the load on a single database.

Load balancer distribution algorithm:

HAProxy is configured with the "Round Robin" algorithm. Round Robin
distributes incoming requests to the servers in the pool sequentially,
one after another, in a repeating cycle (Server 1, then Server 2, then
Server 1, then Server 2, and so on). Each server receives an
approximately equal share of the traffic. Other algorithms exist too,
such as Least Connections (send the request to the server currently
handling the fewest active connections) or IP Hash (always send a
given client IP to the same server), but this setup uses Round Robin
because both servers have identical, equal capacity.

Active-Active vs Active-Passive:

This load-balancer setup is configured as Active-Active. This means
both Server 1 and Server 2 are actively processing traffic at the
same time; the load balancer sends requests to both of them
simultaneously, according to the distribution algorithm.

This is different from an Active-Passive setup, where only one server
(the Active one) handles all the traffic at any given time, while the
second server (the Passive one) stays idle, on standby, ready to take
over only if the Active server fails. In Active-Passive, the passive
server's resources are not used for normal traffic, whereas in
Active-Active, both servers' resources are used, which allows for
better usage of resources and higher total capacity, at the cost of
more complex configuration (for example, session/data consistency
between servers).

How a Primary-Replica (Master-Slave) database cluster works:

In a Primary-Replica cluster, one database server is designated as
the Primary (Master), and the other as the Replica (Slave). All write
operations (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE) are sent only to the Primary
database. The Primary then replicates (copies) those changes to the
Replica database, usually asynchronously, so the Replica's data stays
in sync with the Primary shortly after each write. Read operations
(SELECT) can be performed on either the Primary or the Replica,
allowing read traffic to be spread across both databases and reducing
the load on the Primary.

Difference between the Primary node and the Replica node, from the
application's point of view:

The application must send all write queries (data that needs to be
created, updated, or deleted) to the Primary node, because only the
Primary accepts writes. The application can send read queries (data
that only needs to be fetched) to either the Primary or the Replica,
often preferring the Replica to reduce load on the Primary. If the
application mistakenly sends a write query to the Replica, it will
fail or be rejected, since Replica nodes are typically configured as
read-only.

Issues with this infrastructure:

- SPOF (Single Point of Failure):
  The load balancer itself is now a SPOF: if HAProxy fails, users can
  no longer reach either server, even though both servers are still
  healthy. The Primary database is also a SPOF for write operations:
  if it fails, no new writes can be processed until a Replica is
  promoted to Primary (which is not automatic in this simple setup).

- Security issues (no firewall, no HTTPS):
  There is no firewall in this infrastructure, meaning any port on any
  server could potentially be reached from the internet, increasing
  the attack surface (for example, direct access to the database port
  or SSH from anywhere). There is also no HTTPS, meaning all traffic
  between the user and the load balancer travels as plain, unencrypted
  HTTP; this exposes any data sent (including passwords, cookies,
  personal information) to interception (man-in-the-middle attacks).

- No monitoring:
  Without monitoring in place, there is no automated way to know when
  a server, the load balancer, or the database goes down, becomes
  slow, or runs out of resources (CPU, RAM, disk). Issues would only
  be discovered when users start complaining, instead of being caught
  and fixed proactively (or even automatically) before they cause a
  visible outage.
